.TH FACEAUTH.CONF 5 "2026-11" "faceauth 0.10.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
faceauth.conf \- face authentication configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/faceauth/faceauth.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
One
.I key
=
.I value
per line;
.B #
starts a comment. Missing keys use their defaults. Unknown keys and invalid
values are ignored and reported by
.B faceauth doctor
and in the daemon log. The file is read on every request; no restart is
needed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B services
PAM services that may use face authentication, separated by commas or
spaces. Users can disable these for themselves but cannot add others. The
service's PAM stack must also load
.BR pam_faceauth (8);
run
.B faceauth-setup configure
(in the libexec directory) as root after changing this list to add it.
An empty value disables face authentication everywhere. Default:
.BR "sudo, sudo-i, polkit-1, kde-fingerprint" .
Graphical login screens
.RB ( gdm-password ,
.BR sddm ,
.BR lightdm ,
.BR cosmic-greeter )
are only used to unlock the user's locked session, never to log in: a face
login does not provide the password needed to unlock keyrings, KWallet, or
encrypted home directories. On GNOME, listing
.B gdm-password
enables face unlock; when the face is not recognized, the password prompt
appears only after
.BR timeout .
.TP
.B threshold
Largest distance between a live face and an enrolled face that counts as a
match, 0.2 to 0.6. Lower values reject more impostors and more genuine
attempts. Default: 0.45.
.TP
.B required_matches
Consecutive frames that must each contain exactly one matching face, 1 to 10.
A frame with no face, several faces, or another face resets the count.
Default: 3.
.TP
.B timeout
Default authentication timeout in seconds, 1 to 30. Default: 5.
.TP
.B require_ir
.B yes
refuses cameras that are not greyscale-only (IR). Colour cameras can be
fooled by a photograph. Default: no.
.SH SECURITY
Face matching is a convenience factor, not a replacement for a strong
password. faceauth performs no liveness detection; an IR camera raises the
bar considerably but is not proof against a determined attacker.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR faceauth (1),
.BR faceauth-daemon (8),
.BR pam_faceauth (8)
